WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.507
Lesson 3 becomes much clearer when alternating current is read as repeated induction caused by repeated rotation, not as a wave that simply appears by itself.

2
00:00:05.507 --> 00:00:11.013
Rotating coil means continuous orientation change.

3
00:00:11.013 --> 00:00:16.520
The angle between the coil and the magnetic field keeps changing through the turn.

4
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:22.027
Flux linkage means keeps changing.

5
00:00:22.027 --> 00:00:27.533
That changing linkage is what Faraday's law responds to.

6
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:33.040
Alternating output means sign keeps reversing.

7
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.547
Each half-turn flips the orientation story and the induced emf changes sign.

8
00:00:38.547 --> 00:00:44.053
Alternating emf is repeated induction generated by repeated rotation.

9
00:00:44.053 --> 00:00:49.560
Keep this takeaway in view: Alternating emf is repeated induction generated by repeated rotation.

10
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.690
This diagram matters because it lets the learner see that the graph is not a separate topic.

11
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.820
It is the time-history of repeated flux change caused by the rotating coil.

12
00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.950
Mechanical input means rotation.

13
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:06.080
The coil is being turned through the magnetic field.

14
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.210
Electromagnetic bridge means changing flux linkage.

15
00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:14.340
The turning motion continuously updates the flux-through-loop story.

16
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.470
Electrical output means alternating emf.

17
00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:22.600
The induced emf becomes positive and negative in a repeating pattern.

18
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.730
The a.c.

19
00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.860
graph is just the rotating-coil induction story read over time.

20
00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.990
Keep this takeaway in view: The a.c.

21
00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.120
graph is just the rotating-coil induction story read over time.

22
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.810
A repeating-orientation analogy helps because the generator output is periodic for the same reason a wheel pattern repeats: after one full turn the geometry has reset to the same state.

23
00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:50.500
Orientation cycle means state repeats every turn.

24
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:56.190
A full rotation returns the coil to the same geometric relationship with the field.

25
00:01:56.190 --> 00:02:01.880
Half-turn contrast means state reverses midway.

26
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.570
After half a turn the orientation is reversed, so the output sign is reversed too.

27
00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:13.260
Lesson gain means frequency has a physical anchor.

28
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.950
Cycles per second come directly from turns per second.

29
00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:24.640
Generator frequency is a repetition rate set by the turning motion.

30
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.330
Keep this takeaway in view: Generator frequency is a repetition rate set by the turning motion.

31
00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:36.848
Lesson 3 is strongest when the learner separates the cycle-rate job from the emf-size job while still keeping both tied to the same rotating coil.

32
00:02:36.848 --> 00:02:43.367
Cycle bridge means one turn to one cycle.

33
00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:49.885
That connects mechanical rotation rate to electrical frequency.

34
00:02:49.885 --> 00:02:56.403
Time bridge means faster turn means smaller delta(t).

35
00:02:56.403 --> 00:03:02.922
The same flux swing happening faster makes the induced emf larger.

36
00:03:02.922 --> 00:03:09.440
Flux bridge means size depends on change.

37
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.958
No flux swing means no induced emf, even if the coil exists.

38
00:03:15.958 --> 00:03:22.477
Keep these relations visible: f = 1 / T; one full turn gives one full a.c.

39
00:03:22.477 --> 00:03:28.995
cycle; and induced emf magnitude = N delta(Phi) / delta(t).

40
00:03:28.995 --> 00:03:35.513
Use the rotation rate to get the period or frequency, then use the flux-linkage change over the chosen time interval to estimate the induced emf.

41
00:03:35.513 --> 00:03:42.032
Rotation rate controls frequency, and flux-change rate controls induced emf.

42
00:03:42.032 --> 00:03:48.550
Keep this takeaway in view: Rotation rate controls frequency, and flux-change rate controls induced emf.

43
00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:54.341
A dependable Lesson 3 route starts with coil orientation, then follows how the flux linkage is changing, then converts that into the sign and size trend of the induced emf.

44
00:03:54.341 --> 00:04:00.132
Step 1 means orientation.

45
00:04:00.132 --> 00:04:05.923
Where is the coil relative to the magnetic field right now?

46
00:04:05.923 --> 00:04:11.714
Step 2 means flux change.

47
00:04:11.714 --> 00:04:17.506
Is the linkage increasing, decreasing, or momentarily turning around?

48
00:04:17.506 --> 00:04:23.297
Step 3 means emf readout.

49
00:04:23.297 --> 00:04:29.088
Use the changing-linkage story to decide the alternating output.

50
00:04:29.088 --> 00:04:34.879
Read the wave as a rotating-coil story, not as an isolated graph.

51
00:04:34.879 --> 00:04:40.670
Keep this takeaway in view: Read the wave as a rotating-coil story, not as an isolated graph.

52
00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:44.987
The first deep idea is the clean frequency link.

53
00:04:44.987 --> 00:04:49.305
If the coil completes 25 turns each second, the alternating output completes 25 cycles each second.

54
00:04:49.305 --> 00:04:53.622
Mechanical rate means turns per second.

55
00:04:53.622 --> 00:04:57.939
This is the rotation frequency of the coil.

56
00:04:57.939 --> 00:05:02.256
Electrical rate means cycles per second.

57
00:05:02.256 --> 00:05:06.574
For a simple generator, this matches the a.c.

58
00:05:06.574 --> 00:05:10.891
frequency.

59
00:05:10.891 --> 00:05:15.208
Lesson gain means follows directly.

60
00:05:15.208 --> 00:05:19.525
Frequency does not need a separate mystery explanation.

61
00:05:19.525 --> 00:05:23.843
Generator frequency is set by how many full turns happen each second.

62
00:05:23.843 --> 00:05:28.160
Keep this takeaway in view: Generator frequency is set by how many full turns happen each second.

63
00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:33.098
The alternating part becomes easier when the learner watches the half-turn carefully.

64
00:05:33.098 --> 00:05:38.036
Midway through the turn the coil has reversed its orientation relative to the field, so the emf direction reverses too.

65
00:05:38.036 --> 00:05:42.974
First half-turn means one emf direction.

66
00:05:42.974 --> 00:05:47.912
The changing flux linkage produces one sign of output.

67
00:05:47.912 --> 00:05:52.850
Second half-turn means opposite emf direction.

68
00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:57.788
The reversed orientation flips the induced direction.

69
00:05:57.788 --> 00:06:02.726
Result means alternating output.

70
00:06:02.726 --> 00:06:07.664
The generator naturally produces positive and negative halves.

71
00:06:07.664 --> 00:06:12.602
Alternation comes from the repeated reversal of coil orientation.

72
00:06:12.602 --> 00:06:17.540
Keep this takeaway in view: Alternation comes from the repeated reversal of coil orientation.

73
00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:23.161
A speed comparison is the strongest numerical bridge in Lesson 3 because it shows why frequency and induced emf often rise together in a generator.

74
00:06:23.161 --> 00:06:28.782
Frequency effect means more cycles each second.

75
00:06:28.782 --> 00:06:34.403
More turns each second means more alternating cycles each second.

76
00:06:34.403 --> 00:06:40.024
Emf effect means larger delta(Phi) over delta(t).

77
00:06:40.024 --> 00:06:45.646
The same flux-linkage swing squeezed into less time increases the induced emf.

78
00:06:45.646 --> 00:06:51.267
Comparison habit means track time explicitly.

79
00:06:51.267 --> 00:06:56.888
The safest route is always to ask how the time interval changed.

80
00:06:56.888 --> 00:07:02.509
Faster rotation means faster cycling and larger induced emf.

81
00:07:02.509 --> 00:07:08.130
Keep this takeaway in view: Faster rotation means faster cycling and larger induced emf.

82
00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:12.974
This hardware scene matters because the learner should not treat the generator as only a rotating diagram.

83
00:07:12.974 --> 00:07:17.817
The output has to leave the coil, and the connection method has to preserve the alternating behavior.

84
00:07:17.817 --> 00:07:22.661
Slip rings means maintain rotating contact.

85
00:07:22.661 --> 00:07:27.505
They stay connected to the turning coil ends.

86
00:07:27.505 --> 00:07:32.348
Brushes means carry the output away.

87
00:07:32.348 --> 00:07:37.192
They let the external circuit receive the emf.

88
00:07:37.192 --> 00:07:42.035
Lesson gain means a.c.

89
00:07:42.035 --> 00:07:46.879
stays alternating.

90
00:07:46.879 --> 00:07:51.723
The connection does not turn the output into d.c.; it preserves the alternating pattern.

91
00:07:51.723 --> 00:07:56.566
The connection hardware should support the alternating output rather than hide it.

92
00:07:56.566 --> 00:08:01.410
Keep this takeaway in view: The connection hardware should support the alternating output rather than hide it.

93
00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:06.450
Use this checkpoint to test whether the learner has tied electrical frequency to mechanical repetition instead of to a memorized graph shape.

94
00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:11.490
Question means double the rotation rate.

95
00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:16.530
What happens to cycles per second in a simple generator?

96
00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:21.570
Route means one turn gives one cycle.

97
00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:26.610
More turns each second means more repeated electrical cycles.

98
00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:31.650
Answer check means frequency doubles.

99
00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:36.690
The electrical repetition rate follows the mechanical repetition rate.

100
00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:41.730
Generator frequency follows the turning frequency directly.

101
00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:46.770
Keep this takeaway in view: Generator frequency follows the turning frequency directly.

102
00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:51.821
Carry one route into the worked example.

103
00:08:51.821 --> 00:08:56.872
Use the rotation rate to find the a.c.

104
00:08:56.872 --> 00:09:01.922
frequency, then find the chosen turn or half-turn time, then apply Faraday's law to the flux-linkage change over that time.

105
00:09:01.922 --> 00:09:06.973
Step 1 means frequency.

106
00:09:06.973 --> 00:09:12.024
Use turns per second to get cycles per second.

107
00:09:12.024 --> 00:09:17.075
Step 2 means time interval.

108
00:09:17.075 --> 00:09:22.125
Choose full-turn or half-turn time carefully.

109
00:09:22.125 --> 00:09:27.176
Step 3 means Faraday.

110
00:09:27.176 --> 00:09:32.227
Use the flux-linkage change over that time.

111
00:09:32.227 --> 00:09:37.278
Keep these relations visible: one full turn gives one full cycle; find the relevant time interval from the rotation rate; and induced emf magnitude = N delta(Phi) / delta(t).

112
00:09:37.278 --> 00:09:42.328
Treat the mechanical rate and the induction calculation as linked stages of one generator story.

113
00:09:42.328 --> 00:09:47.379
Generator questions become manageable when rotation and induction are handled in that order.

114
00:09:47.379 --> 00:09:52.430
Keep this takeaway in view: Generator questions become manageable when rotation and induction are handled in that order.
